The random cliff at $k \in [0.85, 0.925]$ is task-specific: Object collapses from 69.4\% ($k{=}0.95$) to 5.0\% ($k{=}0.85$), whereas Spatial retains 58.4\% at $k{=}0.85$ ($\Delta{=}53$\,pp between suites). The cliff pattern suggests Object manipulation concentrates critical information in specific token subsets whose removal is catastrophic at narrow retention bands; Spatial tasks distribute critical information more uniformly, tolerating moderate random removal without catastrophic failure.

L2 norm selection is consistent across suites at high retention ($k \geq 0.85$): Object and Spatial differ by $\leq 2.4$\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:pi05_crosssuite}). At $k \leq 0.7$, both suites exhibit the L2 V-shape (Object valley: 33.4\% at $k{=}0.5$; Spatial valley: 55.6\% at $k{=}0.5$), with Spatial maintaining higher absolute SR throughout. The method preference (random $>$ L2 at $k{=}0.5$; L2 $>$ random at $k{=}0.3$) is consistent across suites, confirming that the CV-strategy interaction generalizes beyond a single task distribution.

